Request for Software Development (Bandwidth-Optimized)

Project: Multi-Drone Control & Monitoring Application (Binary Protocol)

Objective:
Develop a feature-rich PC application (preferably in Python) that connects via Bluetooth to multiple QuayeWorks Hawk-H7-based drones. The goal is to minimize Bluetooth bandwidth usage by employing a compact binary protocol for telemetry and control data. This application should facilitate real-time telemetry monitoring, GPS mapping, multi-drone distance calculations, parameter tuning, and (optionally) basic mission planning for collaborative flight scenarios—while prioritizing efficient data transfer.

1. Scope & Features
Multi-Drone Bluetooth Connectivity

The application must handle simultaneous connections (up to five drones), each mapped to a unique COM port or serial device on the PC.
A user-friendly interface to label and manage each drone connection (e.g., “Drone A,” “Drone B,” etc.).
Emphasis on minimal bandwidth: standard baud rate or custom optimization to ensure stable, low-latency data exchange.
Binary Protocol for Reduced Overhead

Replace plain-text/JSON telemetry with a compact binary format (e.g., fixed-size packets or TLV structures).
Firmware and PC software must define and adhere to the same packet structure (for GPS data, sensor readings, CPU load, etc.).
Include data integrity measures (checksums, CRC) without significantly increasing packet size.
GPS Position Tracking & Mapping

Store GPS coordinates in binary format (e.g., 32-bit floats or scaled integers).
Display each drone’s position on a map (via embedded web-based maps or an offline library such as Folium).
Compute distances between drones in real time for collaborative/heavy-lift missions.
Sensor Telemetry Dashboard

Show minimal but essential sensor data: IMU, barometer, magnetometer, current, voltage, CPU load, etc.
Provide real-time updates in a graph or numeric display with the smallest necessary data packets.
Calibration & Parameter Tuning

Adjust flight parameters (PID gains, thresholds) via binary configuration messages to each drone.
Save/load multiple tuning profiles.
Include a fallback or sanity checks for corrupted or incomplete packets.
Multi-Drone Coordination

Calculate real-time distances between drones.
Optionally alert when drones approach or exceed predefined distance thresholds.
Mission & Waypoint Features (Optional/Future)

If included, maintain a binary-based command structure for uploading waypoints or flight paths.
Provide feedback when drones accept or complete each waypoint.
Logging & Replay

Save incoming binary telemetry streams to a file (with a timestamp) for post-flight analysis.
Reconstruct data offline and convert to readable formats if needed (CSV or partial JSON).
Safety & Alerts

Minimal but crucial alerts for low battery, proximity warnings, or geofence breaches.
Possibly broadcast an emergency landing or “Return to Home” command to any or all drones.
User Interface & Experience

A lean interface that prevents bandwidth overuse—don’t request high-frequency data beyond what is strictly necessary.
Provide a manual rate-limit or refresh interval setting (e.g., “Update sensor data every 500ms”).
2. Technical Requirements
Language & Framework

Python 3, with recommended libraries:
pyserial (for the underlying Bluetooth serial connections).
A lightweight GUI framework (PyQt / PySide / Tkinter / PySimpleGUI).
A mapping solution (Folium or embedded map) that doesn’t excessively burden system resources.
Binary Data Format

Define a custom packet structure (e.g., a header with packet ID, length, CRC; followed by payload).
Pack numeric fields (e.g., 16-bit or 32-bit integers/floats) carefully to handle GPS, sensor data, etc.
Rely on CRC or checksums to detect corrupted data while keeping overhead minimal.
Threading or Async Handling

Must handle multiple serial ports concurrently.
The UI should remain responsive, updating data only as packets arrive rather than continuously querying.
System Requirements

Runs on Windows 10 or later with a Bluetooth adapter.
Cross-platform deployment (Linux/macOS) is a bonus.
Scalability & Extensibility

Support up to five drones; code should be adaptable for more drones with minimal modifications.
Structured to allow future expansions (e.g., new sensor types or commands) without re-engineering the entire protocol.
3. Deliverables
Source Code & Documentation

A Python codebase with well-commented modules.
Detailed specification of the binary protocol (field sizes, packet types, CRC method).
User Guide

Clear instructions for pairing each drone via Bluetooth, setting up serial ports, and configuring minimal bandwidth usage (e.g., refresh rates).
Release Package

Optionally, a bundled desktop application (e.g., PyInstaller-based) for non-technical operators.
Example Logs & Testing Tools

Sample logs in the chosen binary format, along with a parsing script for validation.
Basic test harness or simulator to inject pseudo-telemetry data for debugging.
4. Proposed Timeline & Milestones
Week 1–2: Protocol & Architecture Design

Define binary packet structures, error-checking approach, and GUI mockups.
Align with firmware constraints.
Week 3–5: Core Communication & Telemetry

Implement multi-threaded serial reading with binary parsing.
Display minimal telemetry in a dashboard.
Week 6–7: Mapping & Distance Calculations

Integrate map component.
Compute and display distances between drones.
Week 8–9: Parameter Tuning & Optional Features

Add GUI for flight parameter adjustment (PID, offsets, thresholds).
Implement logging and replay.
Week 10: Testing, Optimization & Documentation

Field tests to ensure stable connections under bandwidth constraints.
Final refinements, packaging, and user documentation.
(Schedule is tentative and may adjust based on developer availability or scope changes.)

5. Additional Notes
Firmware Coordination: The drone’s firmware must be updated or designed to send/receive data in the agreed-upon binary format—particularly important for sending reduced-size telemetry.
Refresh Rate Tuning: Provide an option to adjust data rates to match real flight needs while remaining within Bluetooth’s bandwidth constraints.
Security: If needed, consider minimal overhead encryption or authentication, though that may affect CPU usage and packet size.
Offline/Peer-to-Peer: For multi-drone collisions or heavy-lift missions, ensure each drone’s data is reliably received in near-real time. If range or reliability is a concern, consider a fallback or emergency protocol.
Conclusion
We seek an efficient, bandwidth-conscious PC application for real-time multi-drone control and monitoring. By implementing a binary protocol with minimal overhead, the software should reliably handle simultaneous Bluetooth links, display telemetry data, support parameter tuning, and compute drone-to-drone distances—all while respecting strict bandwidth and performance considerations. Your proposal detailing development methodology, experience with binary data protocols, and UI design approach would be greatly appreciated.
